## Seat note: stage W (#22895) contract review PASS · one text-only round before it lands · the package door is measured next as W2 · epic PM `session_01Rerax7QTjKMPCUZxQUtPFR` (`marchtian`) · 2026-10-11T22:37Z

**#22895:** the contract review passed, 6114668919, on `5354b1601`. All 42 checks are green. The record found every catalog write door covered:
- REST save, reset, publish and rollback;
- the dispatcher `PUT`;
- the clone door;
- the data door on the three catalog tables.

The activation door was already operator-only.

**One text-only round first** (③ item 4). The changeset is release text, and correcting it after merge would be an edit to a merge-base changeset (#17712). So it is fixed now:
- the data-door bullet names ADR-0090 D12's delegated `authorEnvironmentSets` scope, which can no longer author environment sets under a wall;
- the `capability` FROM at `/meta` is made exact, because that type was already refused there on `main`;
- the ADR-0087 entry's `surface` gives the dispatcher `PUT` only.

The new head gets a delta contract review, then the seat lands it. It is not governed.

**W2, measure first (③ item 9).** By code read, the package door asks `requireManageMetadata` and no posture rung. A package's positions, permission sets and capabilities register as catalog items. Under a wall, a holder below the operator rung might bring catalog definitions in by installing a package, which D3 reserves to the operator.

The same developer measures this after the text round:
- who can install, upgrade or uninstall;
- whether the items register environment-wide and can replace or shadow a name;
- whether it is cross-tenant;
- the operator and `single` controls;
- where a refusal would sit.

Whether walled `manage_metadata` may author objects, views and flows stays with the maintainer. The measurement reports what is reachable and decides nothing. If W2 measures a cross-tenant write, it joins release-cut condition (f).

